  At a fixed alert budget the better RANKER wins. A signal that only
  matches 'random' is carrying no information about the outcome.
  WATCH THE TIGHTEST BUDGETS. A real ranker is BEST at the top; a signal
  that only wins at loose budgets and collapses at 1-2% is over-scaling
  its extreme predictions, not ranking well.

  Concentration of the model's top 26 alerts:
    distinct series 4 | distinct days ~14
    KXBTCD                   22 alerts   12 confirmed (54.5%)
    KXMCHADESII               2 alerts    0 confirmed (0.0%)
    KXHURCAT                  1 alerts    0 confirmed (0.0%)
    KXMCPOKEMONLEGENDSZA      1 alerts    0 confirmed (0.0%)
    excluding 'KXBTCD' (357 events dropped): stale 0.0%  model 0.0%  lift nanx
    -> if the lift collapses here, the edge was that one family.

  Bootstrap over events (2000 resamples, alert budget 119):
    lift            5th 1.40x   median 2.07x   95th 3.40x
    precision diff  5th +5.0pp  median +11.8pp  95th +19.3pp
    model beats stale in 99.9% of resamples
    -> lift interval excludes 1.0. The effect survives resampling.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  events     = both rungs quoted at t, model read BOTH (each hidden in some pass),
               and the pair repriced by t+h so the outcome is adjudicated
  confirmed  = fresh prices at t+h still show a violation
  base rate  = what you get by alerting at random. The floor.
  stale prec = precision of the ARITHMETIC rule on stale prints at t
  model prec = precision of the MODEL's estimated gap, at the SAME alert count
  lift       = model precision / stale precision. >1 means the model
               identifies real arbitrage that arithmetic cannot see.

  VERDICT: the model beats stale arithmetic at 5 of 5 horizons.
  This is the project's central claim, on held-out data: the STGAT identifies
  no-arbitrage violations that survive the market's own adjudication, where
  the naive comparison of last prints does not. Check it holds on VAL too,
  and report precision at matched alert counts, never raw counts.
